## Why do ecommerce sites need specialist SEO?

Ecommerce sites need specialist SEO because they behave differently from blogs, brochure sites and SaaS platforms. A typical online store has thousands of product pages, dozens of category and subcategory pages, filtered navigation that generates hundreds of URL parameters and seasonal stock changes that create constant indexing work.

Generic SEO advice rarely accounts for this. An ecommerce SEO consultant understands how crawl budget, thin content, duplicate pages and the commercial intent behind every search query interact. Ranking a product category page for a high-intent keyword like "buy running shoes online" needs different technical and content work from ranking a blog post.

Online sales now run at over 28% of total UK retail. Mobile carries the majority of ecommerce traffic. My [UK ecommerce statistics](/blog/uk-ecommerce-seo-statistics/) breakdown covers the platform-specific technical debt behind that figure: duplicate collection URLs, thin category pages and slow product pages. That debt separates stores that convert their organic traffic from stores that rank without seeing the revenue.

I run technical SEO audits with ecommerce platforms in mind. They identify the issues that cost you revenue rather than a generic crawl-tool warning list.

## What ecommerce SEO problems do I check first?

Online stores share a predictable set of problems. The six below are the ones I check first on every ecommerce audit.

| Problem | Why it costs revenue | What I do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Duplicate content from faceted navigation | Filters for colour, size, price and brand generate thousands of near-identical URLs that waste crawl budget on pages that should never be indexed | Canonicalisation, parameter handling and crawl directives so only the right category URLs are indexed |
| Thin or missing category page content | Category pages are the strongest ranking assets for high-volume commercial keywords, yet most are bare product grids with no copy, internal links or structured data | Category briefs, supporting copy and internal links that keep the product grid usable |
| Poor product schema | Product structured data drives price, availability and review rich results, and incomplete markup hands click-through rate to competitors | Accurate Product markup that matches the page and the feed |
| Cannibalisation between product and category pages | Product and category pages competing for the same keyword means neither ranks well | A keyword-to-page map and a clear content hierarchy |
| Orphaned products and broken internal linking | Products dropped from navigation, out-of-stock items returning 404s and weak linking stop authority reaching the pages that earn | Reinstated paths, 404 handling for discontinued lines and [internal linking](/services/internal-linking/) from categories and guides |
| Slow product pages | High-resolution images, third-party review widgets and bloated theme code hurt rankings and conversion | Image, script and theme fixes prioritised by Core Web Vitals field data |

## How should a UK ecommerce brand evaluate an AEO agency before hiring?

**A UK ecommerce brand should ask any AEO agency or consultant how they measure AI visibility, what they change on your product data and what they will not promise. A good answer names a fixed set of buyer prompts checked every month. It also covers work on your product feed, Merchant Center data, product pages and reviews. A promise of ChatGPT inclusion is a red flag.**

AI answers vary from one run to the next. Nobody can guarantee your products appear. Use the questions below on every shortlist. That includes me.

| Question to ask | Good answer | Red flag |
|---|---|---|
| How will you measure AI visibility? | A fixed panel of buyer prompts run in the same assistants each month with results and sources logged | Screenshots of one good answer or a single "AI score" with no method |
| Can you guarantee we appear in ChatGPT or Google AI Overviews? | No. They can show what changed and how often you appeared | Guaranteed inclusion or a guaranteed position |
| What will you do with our product feed and Merchant Center data? | Check titles, attributes, prices, availability and GTINs match the product pages | Product data is not mentioned at all |
| How do you handle structured product data? | Make Product markup accurate and consistent with the page and the feed as basic hygiene | Selling schema or llms.txt as a way to get cited |
| What about reviews? | Help you collect genuine reviews and show them on product pages and trusted review platforms | Buying reviews or offering incentives in exchange for positive ones |
| Which sources do AI answers cite for our products? | A list of the retailers, review sites and publishers cited today plus a plan for the ones you can influence | No research into cited sources |
| How do you report sales and enquiries? | AI referral sessions, orders and enquiries reported separately with the numbers behind each | Only "mentions" or "visibility" with no link to revenue |
| What does it cost and what is excluded? | A fixed price for the audit and a clear monthly scope | Long lock-in contracts with vague deliverables |

My [AI visibility audit](/ai-visibility/) for ecommerce stores is a £1,500 fixed fee. It checks whether your store is mentioned, cited or recommended for an agreed set of buyer questions. It includes a competitor benchmark and a prioritised 90-day plan. Delivery takes 2 weeks.

## Which ecommerce platforms do I work with?

I work with stores on Shopify, WooCommerce and Magento (Adobe Commerce). The same crawl, category and product-data principles apply on other platforms. Each platform fails in its own way. Each has its own audit price and detail page.

| Platform | Audit from | What usually breaks |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shopify | £495 | Enforced /collections/ and /products/ URLs, product-in-collection duplicates, limited robots.txt control, app bloat |
| WooCommerce | £750, audit and fix | Permalink structure, plugin conflicts, attribute and tag archives that create thin pages, caching and CDN clashes |
| Magento | £1,500, up to 10,000 products | Layered navigation URL explosion, configurable and grouped product duplicates, multi-store hreflang, performance |

### Shopify SEO consultant

Shopify gets a store live quickly. Its rigid URL structure and limited technical control create specific SEO problems.

As a [Shopify SEO consultant](/services/shopify-seo-consultant/), I work within the platform's constraints to maximise organic visibility: the enforced `/collections/` and `/products/` URL structure, duplicate content from product-in-collection URLs, limited control over robots.txt (now customisable through the theme file), automatic pagination and theme-level performance. I also advise on which Shopify apps help SEO and which add bloat.

### WooCommerce SEO consultant

WooCommerce offers far more technical flexibility than Shopify. That flexibility cuts both ways. I help store owners configure WordPress and WooCommerce correctly from the start, or fix the mess left by poorly configured plugins.

Common [WooCommerce SEO](/services/woocommerce-seo-consultant/) work includes permalink structures, Yoast or Rank Math settings for product and category pages, attribute archives and tag pages that generate thin content, product schema beyond what plugins generate by default and conflicts between caching plugins, CDN configuration and dynamic pricing.

### Magento SEO consultant

Magento (now Adobe Commerce) powers some of the largest ecommerce operations in the UK. I work with Magento stores that need SEO across large catalogues.

[Magento SEO](/services/magento-seo-consultant/) problems include layered navigation generating huge numbers of indexable URLs, configurable and grouped product pages creating duplicate content, multi-store and multi-language setups that need correct hreflang and performance on a resource-heavy platform. I also work with Magento's built-in canonical settings and meta robots configuration to keep indexation clean across the catalogue.

## What does my ecommerce SEO service include?

My ecommerce SEO service starts with a technical SEO audit of your store and a prioritised action plan based on revenue impact. Here is what I typically deliver:

**Technical SEO foundations.** Crawl budget optimisation, canonicalisation strategy, XML sitemap configuration for products and categories, robots.txt refinement, Core Web Vitals improvements and structured data implementation (Product, BreadcrumbList, FAQPage, Organization).

**Category page strategy.** [Keyword research](/services/keyword-research/) to identify the highest-value commercial terms, content briefs for category pages, on-page optimisation and internal linking architecture that channels authority to your most important pages.

**Product page optimisation.** Unique product descriptions, correct schema markup, image optimisation and cross-linking between related products and relevant category pages.

**Content strategy for topical authority.** Stores that invest in supporting content such as buying guides, comparison pages and how-to articles build topical authority that lifts the whole domain. I create content strategies that directly support your product and category rankings.

**Ongoing consulting.** Monthly reporting, priority adjustments based on performance data and strategic guidance as your catalogue evolves.

